From b3038ebd65e79898281b475824e61cd7a22ce095 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 21:00:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] kanban dispatcher: gate reclaim sub-sweeps on the
 content revision or a data-derived due time (G2)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Card t_01a1d59e58887bd2, design t_01a1d563324d7e8e §3 (G2 row), §9 C12, review R13.

Why: the dispatcher's reclaim phase was 5.5% of the hub gateway's samples. A replay of the hub `default`
board (290 MB, 20k runs, 375k events) cost 168-225 ms CPU per 15 s tick, ~1.1-1.5% of a core, on a board
whose content changed on 58-72% of ticks. Claim/lease expiry is bookkeeping (it never bumps the revision,
`kanban_activity_revision.BOOKKEEPING_COLUMNS`), so a revision gate alone would sleep through expiry and
process death. Each gated sweep therefore also carries a due time derived from its own rows, and the
liveness sweeps stay every-tick but start from partial indexes so their empty case is O(candidates).

What:
- `kanban_sweep_gate` (new): `gated(name, conn, fn, extra=, due=, run_due=)` runs `fn` when the key
  (file identity + `kanban_activity_revision` counter + `extra`) is unknown or moved, or when a due time
  arrived: `due(conn)` is re-read every tick (bookkeeping clocks), `run_due(result)` is the next moment the
  run's own rows go due (stored with the key: those rows cannot change while the key holds). A failed run,
  an unreadable counter or a key read inside a transaction never vouches. `memo()` for pure structural reads.
  A skip counts in loop accounting (`skips`).
- `foreign_ids` memoized on the revision (it scanned every task ~7x per tick).
- Shared-blocker lanes (release, probe) gated; due = probe-interval end of each deferred blocker, and the
  14-day lookback exit of the oldest assertion. `idx_runs_blocker_assert` and a JSON-free `run_effect` fast
  path cut a forced `blocker_states` pass from 41 to ~16 ms.
- Continuation parks gated; due = the earliest pending park wake.
- `candidate_task_ids` (archive relay / unresolved archives / guard notice) driven from the event-kind index
  instead of one probe per done/archived task (~5 ms a call, 5 calls a tick).
- `detect_crashed_workers` (`crash_sweep_due`) now also runs while any card holds a local `worker_pid`
  (process death: `_pid_alive`), never gated.
- Partial indexes for the every-tick liveness sweeps: `idx_tasks_claim_lock`, `idx_runs_worker_pid`,
  `idx_runs_rate_limited` (also in the drifted-table rebuild spec).
- `wake_episode.stale_seconds` reads config read-only (no per-tick deepcopy).
- `scripts/kanban_reclaim_replay_bench.py`: replay benchmark over a board copy (`--replay S` replays the
  board's own write cadence; all side effects stubbed).

Every `dispatch_once` sub-sweep (order of `_run_reclaim_phase`, then maintenance):

| sweep | gate key | due time | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| reap_worker_zombies | every tick | - | process liveness (waitpid), 0.005 ms |
| reap_terminal_workers | every tick | - | process liveness; `idx_runs_worker_pid` |
| stopped_scope_units | every tick | - | systemd scope liveness |
| apply_remote_archives | candidates memo (tasks+events rev) | - | per-card retry clocks run every tick on candidates only |
| release_stale_claims (orphan/expired claims) | every tick while a card is `running` | `claim_expires` | bookkeeping clock: never revision-gated |
| reconcile_orphaned_running / close_dangling_runs | every tick | - | liveness; 0.8 ms |
| release_leftover_local_claims | every tick | - | `idx_tasks_claim_lock`, 0.03 ms |
| detect_stale_running / enforce_max_runtime | every tick while running | heartbeat / runtime deadlines | bookkeeping clocks |
| detect_crashed_workers | every tick while running, pending release, or a local `worker_pid` | - | process death (C12) |
| dispatcher_drain (supervisor) | every tick | - | `supervisor_requests` is not a revision table; 0.4 ms |
| wake checks (`run_due_wake_checks`) | every tick | `task_wake_checks.next_at` | already due-driven; probes off-thread |
| continuation parks | revision + own gateway | earliest park wake | gated |
| recompute_ready | every tick | - | external input (GitHub review release, replica freshness, 600 s parent-missing clock); 2.7 ms |
| shared blockers: release | revision + gateway + config | lookback exit | gated |
| shared blockers: probe | revision + gateway + config | probe-interval ends, lookback exit | gated |
| wake-check `foreign_ids` | revision memo (`created_on` is content) | - | memo |
| provider walls `tick_upkeep` | every tick | - | 0.09 ms (walls expire on clocks) |
| quota-block recovery | every tick (keeps its input and cadence) | - | 0.06 ms with the memoized `foreign_ids` |
| maintenance: blocked-card policy, guard holds, quota drain, parent-unclaimed, pickup stall | every tick | - | pickup stall reads Board/remote pickup (external); 2 ms total |
| worker_pickup / placement | spawn phase, not reclaim | - | unchanged |

Benchmark (`scripts/kanban_reclaim_replay_bench.py`, hub `default` snapshot, 15 s interval, CPU per tick):

| shape | base 0ef3eae4ad7 | this change |
|---|---|---|
| replay of the last 30 min (68 of 120 ticks write) | 167.8 ms (1.12% core) | 40.5 ms (0.27%) |
| write-free | 179-199 ms | 23.5 ms (0.16%) |
| a content write every tick (worst case) | 224.7 ms | 54.1 ms (0.36%) |
| hub `hermes` board replay (write-free) | 3.5 ms | 3.0 ms |

Budget (<=0.4% of a core) holds even in the worst case.

Tests: tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_sweep_gate.py (new). RED proofs by mutation:
- expired claim on a write-free board reclaimed within one tick: RED when the liveness branch is gated on
  the revision (`has_running and gated(...)`);
- SIGKILLed worker on a quiet board detected within one tick: RED when `detect_crashed_workers` is gated;
- continuation park and stranded-blocker probe run at their due time with no other change: RED with
  `run_due=None`;
- quota recovery still fires: regression guard (its trigger, another card's completion, is a content write).
`test_kanban_watcher_idle_work::test_empty_blocker_sweeps_skip_and_new_candidates_run` now expects a lane
with candidates to run once, then again only after a content write.
`test_kanban_restart_survival` fails identically on base on the worker host (subprocess readiness timeout).
